  "request": {
    "model": "nomic-embed-text:latest",
    "input": [
      "Synthetic nurse note for software testing. No real patient data.\n\nAt a scheduled commercial-driver medical, the driver answers questions in several stages because a dispatcher keeps calling about the next route, while the nurse checks a current clinical document against a copied workplace summary; an observation described confidently in conversation is kept separate from a finding actually recorded in an identified examination.\n\nThe current GP and medication review confirm established type 2 diabetes managed by diet and exercise alone, with no glucose-lowering medicine or insulin prescribed, and document that driving-relevant comorbidities have been assessed with no disqualifying effect; the driver mentions that an uncle uses injections, but those medicines belong to the uncle, and a previous referral proposing tablets was never implemented as a current treatment change.\n\nThe driver alternates urban deliveries with a longer motorway leg and describes a busy depot where engine noise, glare and interrupted meals can complicate an account of symptoms; the nurse records the work setting as context, while keeping the dated clinical findings distinct from the driver's explanation for why a symptom might have occurred on that shift.\n\nFor the occupational reviewer, the nurse retains both the definite findings and the limitations of the available account, including the timing and testing conditions where they matter, rather than producing a single reassuring label; the driver is told which information is on file and which details still require confirmation, without replacing the recorded clinical evidence with an employer's preference.\n"
    ],
    "truncate": false
  },
  "provider_called": true,
  "status": "completed",
